  "image_name": "45248f4d4cc78344f0e53c496774eea1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a conventional front-side power delivery layout with a newer backside power delivery approach in a silicon chip. In the top schematic, both signal routing and the FSPDN share the front-side metal stack above the transistors on the Si substrate, so the same region carries interconnects and power. In the middle, buried power rails (BPR) are added within or below the device layer, and vertical connections begin linking the upper metal network to these rails.\n\nThe bottom schematic shows the key change: after wafer thinning, the power delivery network is moved to the backside. Power is routed through backside metal layers and reaches the device layer through nanoscale through-silicon vias (nTSVs) that land on the buried power rails. This leaves the front side mainly for signal wiring.\n\nOverall, the diagram highlights a structural evolution from shared front-side routing to separated signal and power paths. The visible message is that backside power delivery, enabled by BPR and nTSVs, frees front-side routing resources and creates a cleaner division between signal interconnects and power distribution.",
